    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Right, sorry about that, got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Okay, let's get back to the math.\n\nFor live load calculation, the American Society of Civil Engineers (ASCE) recommends using a factor of 40 psf for residential decks in Vancouver. This factor takes into account the weight of people, furniture, and other objects that will be using the deck.\n\nTo calculate the live load, you'll need to know the number of people using the deck, as well as the weight of any furniture or other objects that will be placed on the deck. A good rule of thumb is to assume 40 psf for each person, plus 10 psf for each piece of furniture. For example, if you're expecting 10 people on the deck, that's 400 psf (10 people x 40 psf/person). Add to that the weight of any furniture, such as tables, chairs, or planters, and you'll get your total live load.\n\nFor furniture weight, a common estimate is to use 10-20 psf per piece, depending on the size and weight of the item. So, for example, a small table might weigh around 20-30 psf, while",
