      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For furniture weight, a common estimate is to use 10-20 psf per piece, depending on the size and weight of the item. So, for example, a small table might weigh around 20-30 psf, while a larger piece of furniture like a sectional sofa might weigh 50-100 psf or more. You'll want to add these values to your live load calculation to get a total.\n\nNow, about those common mistakes... One of the most critical errors is improper joist spacing. If your joists are too far apart, they won't be able to support the weight of the deck, leading to sagging or even collapse. A good rule of thumb is to space joists 16 inches on center (o.c.) for a standard residential deck. However, if you're building a deck with a lot of heavy furniture or a large number of people, you may need to space them closer together.\n\nAnother common mistake is incorrect beam sizing. If your beams are too small, they won't be able to support the weight of the deck, leading to failure. A good rule of thumb is to use beams that are at least 2-3 times the total load that the beam will be subjected to.",
